ion; Universal Basic Education (UBE); girl-child education | Global literacy; SDG 4 |\n   162\t| p5-socs-11 | Nigeria and Africa — ECOWAS and the African Union | ECOWAS founding, purpose, and member states *(verified at build time — no fixed count in brief)*; AU; regional cooperation | — |\n   163\t| p5-socs-12 | Nigeria and the World — United Nations | UN founding and purpose; key bodies (General Assembly, Security Council, UNICEF); Nigeria's participation | — |\n   164\t| p5-socs-13 | Cultural Diversity and National Unity | Ethnic/religious diversity as strength; NYSC; national integration | Multicultural societies globally |\n   165\t| p5-socs-14 | Leadership and Good Governance | Qualities of a good leader; corruption named and defined (age-appropriate); role of citizens in accountability | Anti-corruption bodies (ICPC/EFCC, simply described) |\n   166\t| p5-socs-15 | Revision and Assessment — P5 Social and Citizenship Studies | Consolidation of P5 topics; bridge to P6 | — |\n   167\t\n   168\t### P6 Social and Citizenship Studies (15 modules)\n   169\t\n   170\t| ID | English title | Core SCS strand | Sensitive gate |\n   171\t|----|--------------|-----------------|----------------|\n   172\t| p6-socs-01 | Globalisation and Its Effects | What globalisation is; technology, trade, culture mixing; effects on Nigeria — opportunities and challenges | — |\n   173\t| p6-socs-02 | Human Rights — Deeper Study | Categories of rights (civil, political, economic, social, cultural); what a rights violation looks like; how citizens seek redress | International human-rights bodies |\n   174\t| p6-socs-03 | Gender Equality and Women's Empowerment | *(Sensitive — user sign-off on tone before drafting — see §8)* | — |\n   175\t| p6-socs-04 | Sustainable Development | Meaning; three pillars (economic, social, environmental); examples from Nigeria | SDGs overview |\n   176\t| p6-socs-05 | Electoral Process — Deeper Study | Voter registration, campaigns, voting day, counting, results announcement, petition process; INEC roles | — |\n   177\t| p6-socs-06 | The Nigerian Judiciary — Protecting Rights | Courts and their roles: Magistrate Courts handle many first cases; High Courts hear important matters and some appeals; Court of Appeal reviews specified decisions; Supreme Court of Nigeria is the highest court. Nigeria also has parallel state, Sharia and customary court structures — present this honestly rather than as one simple ladder | — |\n   178\t| p6-socs-07 | Social Cohesion — Inter-ethnic and Inter-religious Tolerance | Historical coexistence; shared civic identity; dialogue and mediation bodies | — |\n   179\t| p6-socs-08 | Migration — Internal and International | Economic migration; IDPs and refugees in Nigeria and globally; UNHCR (simple); rights of displaced persons | — |\n   180\t| p6-socs-09 | Safety and Security — Staying Safe as a Citizen | *(Sensitive — user sign-off on full Hausa draft required before slice approval — see §8)* | — |\n   181\t| p6-socs-10 | Technology and Society | Mobile phones, internet, social media; benefits and risks; cyberbullying; staying safe online | — |\n   182\t| p6-socs-11 | Civic Role Models in Nigeria | Civic contributions of notable Nigerians — Nana Asma'u, Aminu Kano, Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti, Herbert Macaulay, Wole Soyinka — framed around their civic/community impact, not historical narrative; diverse across gender, region, and era; one-sentence biography maximum, rest is civic contribution | — |\n   183\t| p6-socs-12 | Population Growth and Civic Responsibility | Nigeria's population scale and trajectory; effects on healthcare, food, schools; civic and community responsibility — no undated figures; any statistic must include its reference year and authoritative source | — |\n   184\t| p6-socs-13 | Entrepreneurship and Self-Reliance | Small business; practical skills (tailoring, farming, crafts, trade); role of Bank of Agriculture and BOI *(not NACRDB — institution no longer current)*; why self-reliance strengthens communities | — |\n   185\t| p6-socs-14 | Peace Education and Conflict Prevention | Defining peace; community peace-building; dialogue; role of youth in peacebuilding | UNICEF peace-education framework (simple) |\n   186\t| p6-socs-15 | Revision and Consolidation — Bridge to JSS1 | Full P3–P6 Social and Citizenship Studies summary; civic identity; readiness for Junior Secondary | — |\n   187\t\n   188\t---\n   189\t\n   190\t## 7. Sensitive-topic controls (per module)\n   191\t\n   192\t### `p5-socs-07` — Drug Abuse (user sign-off required before slice approval)\n   193\t\n   194\t- Harmonise with the existing AJAMIX safety treatment standard (non-stigmatising, age-appropriate, NAFDAC framing).\n   195\t- Named substances limited to: `taba` (tobacco/cigarettes), `giya` (alcohol), and medicines not approved or provided by a parent, doctor, or health worker.\n   196\t- **Prohibited:** illicit-drug catalogue, street names, methods of use, procurement, concealment, or intoxication descriptions.\n   197\t- Frame around: what drug misuse is, why it is harmful to health and family, and how to refuse peer pressure and seek help from a trusted adult.\n   198\t- Codex drafts full Hausa text; Architect flags it explicitly in the M4 milestone report; user reviews it before M4 is approved.\n   199\t\n   200\t### `p6-socs-09` — Safety and Security (user sign-off required before slice approval)\n   201\t\n   202\t- **Title changed** from \"Terrorism and Security\" to \"Safety and Security — Staying Safe as a Citizen.\" Content teaches personal and community safety, not counterterrorism awareness.\n   203\t- **Prohibited:** extremist group names, tactics, recruitment narratives, attack methods, current conflict-zone examples, community-level warning signs, profiling by religion, clothing, ethnicity or behaviour.\n   204\t- Focus exclusively on: moving away from immediate danger, not touching suspicious or unknown objects, following official instructions, telling a trusted adult or calling emergency services.\n   205\t- Codex drafts full Hausa text; Architect flags explicitly in M5 milestone report; user reviews before M5 is approved.\n   206\t\n   207\t### `p6-socs-03` — Gender Equality and Women's Empowerment (user sign-off on tone before drafting)\n   208\t\n   209\t- At the start of the P6 slice, Architect surfaces the proposed framing to the user before dispatching Codex.\n   210\t- Content should reflect girls' rights to education and to pursue any lawful occupation; women in Nigerian public, professional, and community life; civic equality.\n   211\t- Do not include prescriptive statements about domestic roles, marriage, or reproduction.\n   212\t- Do not introduce content that conflicts with the learner's religious or cultural context without framing it as civic rights, not cultural critique.\n   213\t\n   214\tFamily planning is excluded from `p5-socs-09`. M0 found no supporting public P5 objective, and the official grade document was unavailable. Reconsider only if that official document later confirms the objective and the user explicitly approves reopening the scope.\n   215\t\n   216\t---\n   217\t\n   218\t## 8. Schema contract\n   219\t\n   220\tEvery new module is an object in `content.json.modules[]` with **exactly these keys**, modeled on existing `p2-socs-01`:\n   221\t\n   222\t```json\n   223\t{\n   224\t  \"id\": \"p3-socs-01\",\n   225\t  \"gradeband\": \"p3\",\n   226\t  \"subject\": \"Social Studies\",\n   227\t  \"subjectHa\": \"Nazarin Zamantakewa\",\n   228\t  \"moduleNumber\": 1,\n   229\t  \"titleEn\": \"<English title>\",\n   230\t  \"titleHa\": \"<Hausa title>\",\n   231\t  \"titleAjami\": null,\n   232\t  \"ajami_validated\": false,\n   233\t  \"textExplanationHa\": \"<120–170 words of natural Hausa. Correct hooked letters ɓ ɗ ƙ. Second person, warm, concrete examples from a Northern Nigerian child's world.>\",\n   234\t  \"textExplanationAjami\": null,\n   235\t  \"audioScript\": \"[INTRO] ... [MAIN] ... [PAUSE 1] <question — must exactly match microPauses[0].questionHa> ... [MAIN] ... [PAUSE 2] <question — must exactly match microPauses[1].questionHa> ... [OUTRO] ...\",\n   236\t  \"audioFile\": \"audio/p3-socs-01.mp3\",\n   237\t  \"imageCard\": \"images/p3-socs-01.png\",\n   238\t  \"microPauses\": [\n   239\t    { \"pauseAtMs\": 90000, \"questionHa\": \"…\", \"correctAnswer\": \"…\", \"options\": [\"…\",\"…\",\"…\"] },\n   240\t    { \"pauseAtMs\": 150000, \"questionHa\": \"…\", \"correctAnswer\": \"…\", \"options\": [\"…\",\"…\",\"…\"] }\n   241\t  ],\n   242\t  \"quizQuestions\": [\n   243\t    {\n   244\t      \"templateHa\": \"…\",\n   245\t      \"answerFormula\": \"<literal correct Hausa answer string>\",\n   246\t      \"variableRanges\": { \"a\": { \"min\": 0, \"max\": 0 }, \"b\": { \"min\": 0, \"max\": 0 } },\n   247\t      \"distractorFormulas\": [\"<wrong>\",\"<wrong>\",\"<wrong>\"]\n   248\t    }\n   249\t  ],\n   250\t  \"track\": \"formal\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
