{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' tools/image-manifest/p3-socs-image-manifest.json\nsed -n '1,220p' tools/image-manifest/p3-socs-image-manifest-part2.json\"","aggregated_output":"[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-01\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-01.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"The Meaning and Importance of Social and Citizenship Studies\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Ma’ana da Muhimmancin Nazarin Zamantakewa\",\n    \"type\": \"illustration\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A Northern Nigerian primary-school courtyard where girls and boys work together: two pupils place litter in a bin, two care for shared books, and two calmly discuss a simple classroom matter with a teacher nearby, showing observation, cooperation, rules, and care for public property.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Haɗin kai\", \"Kayan jama’a\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Show equal participation by girls and boys; no religious, ethnic, political-party, or status symbols; do not portray any child as naturally obedient, disruptive, or dominant because of gender or background.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-02\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-02.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Our Local Government Area\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Yankin Ƙaramar Hukumarmu\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A simple, non-geographic community map connecting a neutral local government (LGA) office to a market, a water point, a school, a health centre, and a clean local road; small diverse groups of residents give information to an office worker and use the services.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Ofishin Ƙaramar Hukuma\", \"Kasuwa\", \"Wurin ruwa\", \"Makaranta\", \"Cibiyar lafiya\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Use no real seal, party colour, candidate portrait, campaign sign, named office-holder, or claim that one authority alone provides every service; depict women and men as residents and workers.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-03\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-03.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Community Needs and Services\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Bukatun Al’umma da Ayyukan Jama’a\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A child-friendly community-needs map with paired need-and-service examples: clean water and a repaired pump, learning and a school, health and a health centre, safe passage and a drained road, plus pupils reporting a problem to an adult rather than repairing hazards themselves.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Ruwa mai tsabta\", \"Hidimar jama’a\", \"Magudanar ruwa\", \"Cibiyar lafiya\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Children must only observe and report; do not show them entering drains, handling tools, repairing pumps, directing traffic, or doing hazardous sanitation work.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-04\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-04.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Transportation — Road and Rail\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Sufuri ta Hanya da Jirgin Ƙasa\",\n    \"type\": \"comparison\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"Two side-by-side transport scenes: a calm paved road with a car, bicycle, and produce truck, and a passenger train moving on a fenced rail line; a separate safe-crossing inset shows a child with an adult stopping and looking both ways away from the tracks.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Hanya\", \"Jirgin ƙasa\", \"Layin dogo\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"No child on the carriageway or rail line, no vehicle collision, no hanging from vehicles, and no crossing in front of a moving train; keep the rail line physically separated from the pedestrian scene.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-05\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-05.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Transportation — Air and Water\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Sufuri ta Sama da Ruwa\",\n    \"type\": \"comparison\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"Two balanced panels: a passenger aircraft at a generic airport with luggage being loaded, and a stable passenger boat on calm water carrying people and sacks of goods within capacity; every boat passenger wears a life jacket and a child sits beside an adult.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Filin jirgin sama\", \"Jirgin ruwa\", \"Rigar kariya ta ruwa\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"No airline logo, national flag, military aircraft, overcrowding, rough water, standing passengers, unaccompanied child, or person without a life jacket; no one should be shown entering water.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-06\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-06.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Communication — Traditional Methods\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Sadarwa ta Hanyoyin Gargajiya\",\n    \"type\": \"illustration\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A respectful Northern Nigerian market-square scene showing a town announcer delivering a community message while a second adult uses a drum or kalangu; nearby women, men, and children listen calmly, with subtle sound lines indicating that the message reaches people nearby.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Mai shela\", \"Ganga ko kalangu\", \"Saƙo\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Avoid caricature, costume-as-spectacle, royal or political insignia, religious hierarchy, alarm or panic, and claims that one drum pattern has a universal meaning; depict the methods as locally agreed communication.\"\n  }\n]\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-07\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-07.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Communication — Modern Methods\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Sadarwa ta Hanyoyin Zamani\",\n    \"type\": \"comparison\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"Four clear panels in a Northern Nigerian home-and-school setting: a family listening to radio news, pupils watching an educational television programme with a teacher, an adult making a telephone call, and a child using a mobile phone beside a trusted adult; a small signal icon shows that access varies by place.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Rediyo\", \"Talabijin\", \"Waya\", \"Wayar hannu\", \"Bayanin sirri\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Show supervised, purposeful device use; no brand logos, social-media interfaces, phone numbers, personal data, harmful messages, political broadcasts, or implication that every household has electricity, signal, or a device.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-08\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-08.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Our Natural Environment\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Muhallin Halitta Namu\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A labeled Northern Nigerian natural landscape with broad land, grasses, scattered trees, low rocks, a modest river or pond, open air, birds, livestock, and wild animals at a safe distance; a small seasonal inset contrasts greener rainy-season vegetation with dry-season ground.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Ƙasa\", \"Ruwa\", \"Iska\", \"Itatuwa\", \"Dabbobi\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Use a realistic but non-alarming dryland landscape; do not depict drought victims, dead animals, disaster, ethnic costume markers, political boundaries, unsafe water collection, or a claim that all Northern Nigerian environments look identical.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-09\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-09.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Caring for Our Environment\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Kula da Muhallinmu\",\n    \"type\": \"comparison\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A before-and-after community scene: one side shows litter near a blocked drain, smoke from waste burning far from people, and a cut tree; the improved side shows adults safely clearing the drain, covered waste collection, newly planted trees, and children placing light litter in a bin under adult supervision.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Kwandon shara\", \"Magudanar ruwa\", \"Dasa wasu itatuwa\", \"Kula da muhalli\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Children must not enter drains, burn waste, handle sharp refuse, use cutting tools, or perform hazardous cleanup; keep smoke non-graphic and do not blame any social group for environmental damage.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-10\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-10.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Food and Farming in Our Community\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Abinci da Noma a Al’ummarmu\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A simple farm-to-market sequence in Northern Nigeria: adult farmers tend millet, guinea corn, beans, and groundnuts; cattle, goats, and poultry have water, feed, and shade; harvested grain is safely dried and stored, then transported to a local market.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Gero\", \"Dawa\", \"Wake\", \"Gyada\", \"Shanu\", \"Awaki\", \"Kaji\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Show women and men in varied farming and trading roles; children may observe or do only light supervised tasks, with no machinery, pesticides, blades, heavy loads, animal distress, overcrowding, or unsafe handling.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-11\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-11.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Market and Trade\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Kasuwa da Ciniki\",\n    \"type\": \"comparison\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A calm local market comparison: one panel shows a seller weighing grain accurately while an adult buyer asks the price and pays money; another shows two adults mutually agreeing to exchange grain for another useful good, with clear arrows indicating goods moving both ways.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Mai sayarwa\", \"Mai saya\", \"Farashi\", \"Kuɗi\", \"Musayar kaya da kaya\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"No real currency detail, brand, price claim, deceptive scale, aggressive bargaining, crowd danger, child handling money alone, gendered market roles, or portrayal of barter as belonging to a backward group.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-12\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-12.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Nigerian Citizenship\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Zama Ɗan Ƙasar Nijeriya\",\n    \"type\": \"illustration\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A diverse group of Nigerian primary pupils in an ordinary school courtyard: one learns with books, one speaks while others listen, two protect shared school property, and children with varied regional clothing details cooperate beneath a small neutral outline of Nigeria.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Ɗan ƙasa\", \"Haƙƙoƙi\", \"Alhaki\", \"Kula da kayan jama’a\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Balance gender, region, language, faith-neutral presentation, and disability inclusion; no party colours, politician, campaign sign, government seal, military imagery, citizenship document, ethnic ranking, or suggestion that one appearance defines a Nigerian citizen.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-13\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-13.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Our Cultural Heritage — Food, Dress, and Language\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Abinci, Tufafi, da Harshe a Al’adunmu\",\n    \"type\": \"illustration\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A respectful Nigerian cultural-learning circle with Northern examples foregrounded: elders and younger people discuss tuwo and soup, a flowing robe and wrapper, and several written language-name cards; additional foods, dress styles, and language cards indicate diversity from other Nigerian regions without turning the scene into a costume display.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Abinci\", \"Tufafi\", \"Harshe\", \"Al’ada\", \"Tuwo da miya\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"No sacred objects, masquerade imagery, caricature, exoticising pose, ethnic hierarchy, or claim that every member of a community eats, dresses, or speaks identically; present all examples with equal visual dignity.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-14\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-14.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Festivals and Special Occasions in Nigeria\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Bukukuwa da Ranaku na Musamman a Nijeriya\",\n    \"type\": \"comparison\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"Four equal, clearly separated, neutral panels labeled Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Christmas, and New Yam: families greeting after a communal gathering, a meal shared with others, a modest Christmas family gathering, and a community presenting newly harvested yams; every panel receives equal size and visual warmth.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Eid al-Fitr\", \"Eid al-Adha\", \"Christmas\", \"New Yam\", \"Ladabi\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Keep the panels factual and equal; no worship reenactment, sacred figure, sacrificial act, blood, denominational symbol hierarchy, ethnic caricature, ranking, 'our versus their' framing, politician, party colour, or implication that New Yam is a nationwide federal public holiday.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-socs-15\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-socs-15.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Revision and Assessment — P3 Social and Citizenship Studies\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Bita da Tantancewa — Nazarin Zamantakewa na Aji Uku\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A child-friendly revision wheel with eight balanced spokes leading from a central learner to local government and public services, transport safety, traditional and modern communication, natural environment and care, farming and food, market trade, citizenship, and cultural festivals.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Ƙaramar hukuma\", \"Sufuri\", \"Sadarwa\", \"Muhalli\", \"Noma\", \"Ciniki\", \"Ɗan ƙasa\", \"Al’ada\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Keep every topic equally weighted and use neutral generic symbols; no political insignia, religious ranking, dangerous transport scene, child labor, environmental hazard handling, exam score, or depiction implying one culture represents all Nigeria.\"\n  }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
